Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author (adapted from the novel by Author)
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Notes: One continuous live-action medium, present-day period (Milwaukee, then Seattle, spanning roughly fifteen years). The narrator is never given a first name until the closing scene, per the book's late reveal — he is credited as THE NARRATOR until then.

FADE IN:

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

A small, warm apartment. A cat, BOZO, curled on the couch. THE NARRATOR — late thirties, lean, tired eyes — sits at a table with a notebook and a mug of tea. He looks older than his years.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Not the guy from that book we read in junior high. My real Big Brother. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of.

He closes the notebook. Stares at nothing.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Milwaukee is where we lived all of our lives. Kirk Street. Fifth floor walk-up. This is that story. This is Nick and me.

EXT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT BUILDING - DAY

A brick five-story walk-up, downtown Milwaukee. NICK — mid-twenties, reddish-brown hair, a puzzle of old scars along his jaw and left eyebrow — hauls six grocery bags up the front steps in one trip. THE NARRATOR, younger here, early twenties, follows with two bags, straining.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls. When he walked in someplace, people moved out of the way.

Nick's burnt-orange 1980 Trans Am, yellow flames on the side, sits at the curb.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
That car. A V8, T-top, only cost him twelve grand. People always looked up when Nick pulled up to the lights in that thing.

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

A cramped corner gym. A LIFTER mid-set on the preacher curl bench drops the bar the second he sees Nick coming and offers up the bench. Nick nods, takes over, loads a 50-pound bar for "warm-ups."

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
He's my brother. I know he'll stand up for me no matter what.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

MA — early sixties, tired eyes, an afghan over her knees — watches a game show. THE NARRATOR sits nearby. Nick stands, restless, checking his phone.

NICK
What's Ma doin' back in the bedroom at this time of day?

THE NARRATOR
I don't know, why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. That's how old people go downhill.

THE NARRATOR
I'll do a better job.

NICK
You don't need a degree to get a good job. All you need is to know somebody.

THE NARRATOR
Could you get me on down at the factory?

NICK
What kind of shit do you want to take at school anyway? Nobody ever learned anything in college you can use in the real world. You're too old anyway.

Narrator says nothing. Ma laughs at the television, oblivious.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR SIDEWALK - DAY

THE NARRATOR walks his bike toward the pizza parlor door. SIONNA — short brown hair, pale skin, light blue eyes, a fish-symbol necklace — trips on the cement and brushes into his shoulder.

THE NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes off her shirt.

THE NARRATOR
I work here. I'm just going to work. What's your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

THE NARRATOR
Sionna? That's different.

SIONNA
No thanks, I don't eat pizza. But if you want to go have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She looks back over her shoulder as she walks away.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like I "knew" her. Like I'd seen her somewhere before.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

A crowded corner bar, TV on the Packers game. THE NARRATOR sits at the bar. NICK slaps him on the back, orders a Jack and Coke.

NICK
Hey, little brother. What's shakin'?

THE NARRATOR
Ma's been acting weird. Confused. Asking about church, about Mrs. Canasta.

NICK
I knew she'd start goin' crazy cooped up in that place. Do somethin' with her before she turns into some demented old fart and we gotta pay for a home. Is that what you want?

THE NARRATOR
Of course not.

NICK
That's more like it.

Narrator pays for his beer, leaves. Outside, he spots the library across the street.

INT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Wood floors, hanging lamps on chains, towering shelves. THE NARRATOR approaches the front desk.

THE NARRATOR
How do I take a book home from here?

LIBRARIAN
Do you have a library card?

She hands him a temporary paper card. He wanders into "classic literature," pulls WUTHERING HEIGHTS off the shelf.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I had no idea what I wanted, but I figured they were all important if they were in that section.

INT. JAVA HUT COFFEE SHOP - DAY

College kids with backpacks and highlighters. SIONNA sits at a back table. THE NARRATOR joins her, awkward.

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

THE NARRATOR
Wuthering Heights. Guy and a girl who grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA
Yeah, I know. I read it.

She talks — ghosts, doubles, the moors, a love that never changes destroying the man who feels it. Narrator listens, transfixed.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It reminded me of the way Nick is. Strong and unwavering. I wondered if it would destroy him too.

EXT. MEDIEVAL FOREST AND FIELD - DAY (DREAM)

Deciduous forest, dappled light. THE NARRATOR — dressed in an embroidered tunic and hose, centuries removed — rides a mare behind a WOMAN with long brown hair. They stop in an open field. He lifts her down. Her face turns — Sionna's face, light blue eyes, ancient clothes.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It is the finest hour of my life. I am falling in love with this woman. I am drowning in her.

They kiss.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR bolts upright, sweating, chest pounding.

THE NARRATOR
Hold!

He stares into the dark.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew that girl. It was Sionna. What is happening to me?

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

NICK storms in, face red, fists clenching.

NICK
Ma missed her doctor's appointment yesterday, you ass. Where the hell were you?

THE NARRATOR
I forgot. I was with someone. I met a girl.

NICK
Oh, great. A girl. Try lookin' at your priorities, Bro.

He slams the door on his way out. Ma sits, unbothered, watching TV.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Would I want Sionna to meet Nick? I can't imagine they could even communicate.

INT. DOCTOR CARSON'S OFFICE - DAY

DR. CARSON, gray-haired, serious, sits across from THE NARRATOR.

DR. CARSON
Your mother's blood pressure is dangerously high. I'm afraid of the risk of a stroke. I want you to take her directly to the emergency room.

THE NARRATOR
Whoa, man. Are you sure it's that bad?

Ma, in the next room, holds her chest.

MA
Someone must be talking about me.

INT. HOSPITAL - MA'S ROOM - NIGHT

Ma sleeps in a hospital bed. THE NARRATOR dozes in a chair, then jerks awake, gasping.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I dreamed I was a soldier. I'd just killed a man. Then I turned, and Nick was running toward me.

He stares at Ma, shaken.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
"Nick! Thank God you're here." And then he stabbed the longest knife I ever imagined into my chest.

A beat. He wipes his face.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Nick had killed me. Why would Nick want to kill me?

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Ma home now, pills organized in a labeled plastic container. NICK sits in the overstuffed chair, boots on the footstool.

NICK
Oh, holy shit. Aren't you nurse Nancy. How'd you come up with this?

THE NARRATOR
Sionna helped me figure it out.

NICK
This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

THE NARRATOR
Stop it, Nick! You haven't even met her. Give her a fuckin' break!

NICK
Don't you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

THE NARRATOR
I'm not a bastard!

NICK
Maybe you are. You're a giant pussy — and that makes you and me way different, Bro.

THE NARRATOR
You're so Goddamn hateful.

Nick's hand cracks across his head. Narrator's ear rings.

NICK
Don't you EVER talk that way about MY mother.

He storms out, slamming the door.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
In that split second, I knew I never wanted Sionna to meet that prick.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

Months later. Pool tables, cue sticks, a rock song blaring. NICK spots LINDSEY, his ex, across the room with a new boyfriend, RAY.

NICK
Who's she with? Who's that asshole?

THE NARRATOR
Drop it, man. She can see whoever she wants.

Nick strides over.

NICK
What's up, Lindsey?

RAY
Who is this?

NICK
Where did you meet this one? We keep our ears out.

RAY
What the fuck is wrong with you? You got a problem with me?

Nick's fists clench, nostrils flare. THE NARRATOR steps between them.

THE NARRATOR
Nick, listen — you don't even know this guy. Leave it, man.

NICK
Keep out of it! You're going to be next if you don't get out of my face.

THE NARRATOR
Stop it! You're crazy as shit! No one cares about your ego, Nick!

Nick's fist draws back — swings. Chaos: Joe and a bouncer rush in, Nick headbutts the bouncer, Lindsey screams. THE NARRATOR runs, out the door, into the street.

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

Breathless, THE NARRATOR sits on Sionna's green suede sofa, telling her everything.

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you. No one who really cares about you does that.

THE NARRATOR
I love Nick though. He's my brother.

SIONNA
Look at all the progress you've made that he hasn't.

He lies with his head in her lap. She strokes his hair.

THE NARRATOR
The first time I met you, it was like I'd finally come home.

SIONNA
Me too, honey. Me too.

They drift asleep on the couch. In a half-dream, Nick appears as a giant with a whirling mace, and Sionna rises from water like an angel, driving him back. THE NARRATOR wakes with a start, nearly hitting her chin.

THE NARRATOR
I had the craziest dream. You were an angel.

SIONNA
That sounds about right.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Weeks later. NICK, in his suede jacket, kisses Ma's cheek.

NICK
So what's up with you?

THE NARRATOR
Not much. Working. Thinking about school.

NICK
Going to school? How the hell are you gonna pay for that?

THE NARRATOR
Low-interest student loan. I already applied. I don't want to work in a pizza place my whole life. I need my family to support me on this.

Nick studies him a long moment.

NICK
Remember Donny Amstel? I followed him home, shoved into his door, told him our family had mafia connections. Worked, right?

THE NARRATOR
It did, Nick. It did.

NICK
You're not just my brother, man. You're my best friend. But bro's before ho's. You get me?

THE NARRATOR
Sure, Nick. I get you.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

SIONNA sets down two plates of pie. NICK looks at her offered hand like it belongs to something alien.

SIONNA
I'm Sionna. Nice to meet you, I've heard so much about you.

NICK
Uh, yea. Likewise.

He refuses the pie.

NICK (CONT'D)
So I hear you're a university lady. What are you gonna do when you're done?

SIONNA
English major, philosophy minor. College professor, I hope.

NICK
Hope you can break through that glass ceiling. Tough for a woman to teach at that level, so I've heard.

SIONNA
I have to disagree, Nick. Bachelor's degrees earned by women have risen from thirty-five to fifty-five percent in the last forty years. We make up sixty percent of undergraduates now. You can't keep a good woman down, so to speak.

Nick, stunned silent, has no comeback. Ma beams at Sionna like she's a saint.

MA
Oh my.

Nick recovers, stands to leave, grips Narrator's shoulders on the way out.

NICK
(whispering, close)
Take care of Ma.

THE NARRATOR
Nick, you know I will.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREET - DAY

Nick's new sedan pulls away with Ma in the passenger seat, dressed for Mass.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The most crazy thing ever — Nick took Ma to church. In my mind I saw lightning bolts hitting the aisle as he genuflected.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - DAY

Morning. THE NARRATOR walks into an empty, silent apartment. No Ma, no purse, no coat. The answering machine light blinks.

ANSWERING MACHINE (V.O.)
You have five missed calls. Mr. Olson, this is Officer Dupuis, Milwaukee County Sheriff's Department. We need to talk to you regarding your family.

He grabs his bike, pedals hard, nearly clips a kid on a skateboard.

INT. MILWAUKEE POLICE STATION - DAY

A stone building, 1898 carved into the plaque. OFFICER DUPUIS, gray-haired, mustached, sits across a desk from THE NARRATOR.

OFFICER DUPUIS
There's been a serious accident involving your brother, Nicholas Olson, and your mother, Olivia Olson. They were hit broadside at Werner and Lowe. Your brother is in serious condition at Frodo Hospital. I regret to tell you your mother passed away at the scene.

THE NARRATOR stares, hands shaking around a Styrofoam cup.

THE NARRATOR
Can I have a ride to the hospital?

INT. HOSPITAL - ICU - DAY

Through a high window, THE NARRATOR sees the top of Nick's head, wires and machines around him. A NURSE waits beside him.

NURSE
Are you up to seeing him?

THE NARRATOR
No. I'm really not up to seeing him. Sorry.

He turns and walks out.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
She was dead. And Nick killed her. He fucking killed my mom.

EXT. ST. MARY'S CEMETERY - DAY

A modest gathering at a graveside. THE NARRATOR throws a handful of dirt onto the maple-colored casket lid. It lands with a muted thunk. SIONNA stands beside him.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Mother: Olivia Anne Olson. I have fought the good fight. I have finished the race. I have kept the faith.

INT. HOSPITAL - NICK'S ROOM - DAY

Five days later. NICK, bruised, sallow, oxygen tube in his nose, a chest tube taped to his side. He clicks off the TV.

NICK
Hey, bro. I guess I really fucked up, man. Can't believe Ma's gone.

Tears well in his eyes.

NICK (CONT'D)
I didn't mean to have three Jack and Cokes before I remembered church. The cops are sayin' vehicular manslaughter. It was a mistake. Why would I want to hurt Ma?

THE NARRATOR
Whose fault was it, Nick?

NICK
What do you mean? You think I meant to kill her?

THE NARRATOR
Think about it, Nick.

He turns and walks out. Nick's face twists behind him.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I no longer gave two shits about Nick. I lost my mom, and it seemed I'd lost my brother too.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT ON WERNER STREET - NIGHT

Weeks later. The apartment is a wreck — papers, bottles, ashtrays. NICK paces, smoking despite himself, staring at a photograph of Ma.

NICK (V.O.)
That little bitch. It's her fault this is all going down. If it wasn't for her messin' with my brother's head, Ma would still be alive and I'd still have my life.

He grinds out the cigarette, grabs his coat.

NICK (V.O.) (CONT'D)
She's going to get a piece of my mind.

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - LIVING ROOM AND HALLWAY - NIGHT

A knock. SIONNA opens the door a crack.

SIONNA
Nick? Is that you? I never would've expected you here.

NICK
Can I talk to you?

He steps inside, uninvited, looming.

SIONNA
Nick, whatever's on your mind, we can work through it. Would you like some coffee?

NICK
I don't want your fucking coffee. You're the reason I'm probably going to jail.

SIONNA
Let's talk about this rationally.

NICK
(advancing, backing her to the wall)
If it wasn't for you, Ma would be alive and I'd be working. You come up with some money — your freaky hippie parents got some — and you get the fuck away from my brother.

SIONNA
Nick, calm down. This has nothing to do with me.

He grabs her by the throat with one hand. She makes a choked, animal sound. With the other hand he flicks open a knife in front of her face.

NICK
This is so you remember how serious I am. No more brother. No more contact. You got that?

The front door slams open.

THE NARRATOR (O.S.)
Holy shit, Nick, what the fuck are you doing?

He lunges for Nick's arm. In the struggle, the blade drives into her shoulder. Blood. Nick shoves an elbow back into Narrator's head and flees down the hall, out the door.

THE NARRATOR
(dropping to her side, tearing her shirt to find the wound)
Hold on. Hold on. I'm calling 911 — hold on, Sionna—

He presses both hands against the wound. Sirens rise in the distance.

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Months later. SIONNA sits in the rocking chair, shoulder healed but stiff, eyes vacant. THE NARRATOR sits across from her.

THE NARRATOR
Siona? What's going on?

SIONNA
I don't know. It's hard to figure out how I'm feeling. Thinking about seeing a therapist.

THE NARRATOR
I feel like you're blaming me. You know it had nothing to do with me, right?

SIONNA
I can't feel me. I can't feel myself anymore.

THE NARRATOR
(kneeling before her)
Please know that I love you. You're safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
As long as there's any doubt, I can't go on with you. Maybe I just need to go to Mom and Dad's for a while.

THE NARRATOR
Siona, don't do that. Please stay. We can work things out.

She rises, walks silently back into the bedroom. He stands alone in the empty living room.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
My fingers were slipping slowly and painfully off the rocky cliff. I had no one now.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Years later. THE NARRATOR — older, the man from the opening — sits with Bozo the cat, a bowl of nuts, a cup of tea. A framed certificate on the wall: TONY'S BOY'S PIZZA.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Seven years since my life suddenly stopped being what it was. Seven years of change. Seven years of hatred for my brother.

He falls asleep in the chair. In the dream, an INDIAN SAGE in white walks beside him on a green, hallucinogenic road. Narrator points at a dirty pizza box on the path.

THE NARRATOR
How could someone leave that dirty box here? It's terrible.

SAGE
Why haven't you learned anything? Stop judging things. Nothing is good or bad. This is an illusion. You create your own reality. Start living the truth.

The sage vanishes. So does the box. THE NARRATOR wakes, Bozo curled against him, grabs a notebook and writes furiously.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I had to change. I had to find forgiveness.

He picks up his phone, books a flight.

INT. MILWAUKEE DELI - DAY

THE NARRATOR eats a Reuben alone. BOB — heavier, grayer — spots him from a nearby table.

BOB
Holy shit. Is that you, bro?

He crosses over, slaps him hard on the back, eyes wet.

BOB (CONT'D)
Look at you, man. Big shoulders. You movin' back?

THE NARRATOR
No, just here for a visit. To see Nick. I haven't seen him for eight years. I thought it was time.

BOB
Wow, dude. You got some cojones. I don't think I could forgive him myself.

THE NARRATOR
That's kind of why I'm here. I need to let the shit go.

BOB
(pulling him into a bear hug)
I'm so proud of you, man. So proud.

EXT. SIONNA'S OLD HOUSE - DAY

THE NARRATOR parks across the street. The porch is bare now — no chairs, no plants.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It made me sad to see it. Like the house had a soul and felt lonely without us.

He sits a long moment, remembering her laugh, then starts the engine.

THE NARRATOR
You can't look back, man. You're moving forward.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE - VISITATION ROOM - DAY

A thick plexiglass divider, two phones. A GUARD stands watch. THE NARRATOR sits. NICK is brought in — gray-haired now, thin, deep bags under his eyes. They stare at each other a long moment before either speaks.

NICK
(voice graveled)
Hi, bro. Glad you came.

THE NARRATOR
Hi, Nick. Good to see you.

NICK
Not a cakewalk, but I got my routine down. Might get out early. Parole hearing in two years.

THE NARRATOR
Good for you, Nick.

A beat.

THE NARRATOR (CONT'D)
I'm here to tell you something, and I need you to just listen. Even though we've been through a lot of shit together — I am no longer interested in seeing you or hearing anything you have to say to me. You're an asshole, Nick. You're an asshole who killed my mother in a drunk driving accident. You held me down my whole life so you could have the money and the exciting life. And you tried to kill the only woman I ever loved, and it drove her away. I'll never see you again because of you.

Nick stares at the counter, saying nothing.

THE NARRATOR (CONT'D)
And I forgive you. I forgive you because it sets me free. It releases your control over me forever. That's what I need.

NICK
(tears sliding down his cheeks, unfettered)
You're so right, man. I did do all that shit. I'm so sorry. I'm so sorry. I wouldn't have come, you know. You're more than I ever was. You deserve to live out there. I deserve to live in here.

GUARD
Time's almost up.

THE NARRATOR
I still believe in you, Nick. I still love you for being my brother. You hear me?

NICK
I hear you, man. I love you too. What are you doin' now?

THE NARRATOR
I own a pizza chain. In Seattle. You always said I couldn't do it. I did it, Nick.

NICK
(stunned, almost smiling)
President Olson. I am so proud of you, man. I love you.

GUARD
Time's up.

NICK
Bye.

He's led away. THE NARRATOR sits holding the receiver a moment longer, then hangs it up gently.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Back in the apartment. Bozo on the couch. THE NARRATOR sets his bag down, exhales, sinks into the chair.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
That part of my life was closed now. And it was a complete relief.

He picks up a boarding pass stub from his coat pocket, glances at the name printed on it.

THE NARRATOR
President Peter Olson.

A small, tired smile.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
That's me.

He looks toward the window — city lights, rain starting against the glass.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

THE END
